Signed-off-by: Axel Lin <axel.lin@ingics.com>
Reviewed-by: Stephen Boyd <sboyd@codeaurora.org>
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Link: http://lkml.kernel.org/r/1431334978.17783.4.camel@ingics.com
Signed-off-by: Thomas Gleixner <tglx@linutronix.de>

Here is the root cause:
The bug was caused by incorrect error handling in the `devm_request_any_context_irq()` function, which did not properly check for negative return values from `request_any_context_irq()`. This led to a situation where failure cases were not correctly identified, potentially resulting in undefined behavior or resource leaks. The function erroneously treated any non-zero return value as a success, which is incorrect since `request_any_context_irq()` returns negative values on failure.

Here is the patch:
  */
 int devm_request_any_context_irq(struct device *dev, unsigned int irq,
 			      irq_handler_t handler, unsigned long irqflags,
 			      const char *devname, void *dev_id)
 {
 	struct irq_devres *dr;
 	int rc;

 	dr = devres_alloc(devm_irq_release, sizeof(struct irq_devres),
 			  GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!dr)
 		return -ENOMEM;

 	rc = request_any_context_irq(irq, handler, irqflags, devname, dev_id);
-	if (rc) {
+	if (rc < 0) {
 		devres_free(dr);
 		return rc;
 	}

 	dr->irq = irq;
 	dr->dev_id = dev_id;
 	devres_add(dev, dr);

-	return 0;
+	return rc;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(devm_request_any_context_irq);


